### Logical Relationships (Edges)

| **Parent → Children** | **Relation / Execution** | **Justification** |
|------------------------|--------------------------|-------------------|
| G1 → G2, G3 | AND ; (sequential) | Collection must finish before delivery can start. |
| G2 → G2.1, G2.2 | OR FALLBACK(G2.1,G2.2) | Normal collection vs battery‑low fallback. |
| G2.1 → AT1, AT2, AT3 | AND ; (sequential) | Navigate → request → wait must occur in order. |
| G2.2 → AT4, AT5 | AND ; (sequential) | Return to recharge then hand off the task. |
| G3 → G3.1, G3.2 | OR FALLBACK(G3.1,G3.2) | Normal delivery vs battery‑low fallback. |
| G3.1 → AT10 | AND (leaf) | Deliver the resources. |
| G3.2 → AT6, AT7 | AND ; (sequential) | Return to checkpoint then hand off remaining work. |
| G4 → AT8, AT9 | AND # (parallel) | Alert and report must both be executed, order irrelevant. |
| G3.2.1 → AT6 | AND (leaf) | Return to checkpoint. |
| G3.2.2 → AT7 | AND (leaf) | Assign remaining delivery task. |
| G4.1 → AT8 | AND (leaf) | Trigger alert. |
| G4.2 → AT9 | AND (leaf) | Send report. |

**Notes on Failure Handling**  
- If AT6 (return to checkpoint) fails, the mission triggers G4 (alert + report).  
- The fallback mechanism in G2 and G3 ensures that low‑battery situations are handled by re‑assigning tasks to other robots, preserving mission continuity.

This goal model captures the hierarchical decomposition, execution semantics, and robot‑task assignments required for the multi‑robot resource delivery mission described.
